   "source": [
    "# 15. LOCATION HEADERS AND SPOOFING"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "raw",
   "id": "c2f7cf85-8cc3-4e9e-8ef1-3114f5e5875a",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "HTTP uses Location and Content-Location headers to specify\n",
    "URIs related to a resource.\n",
    "\n",
    "--------------------------------------------------\n",
    "1. LOCATION HEADER\n",
    "--------------------------------------------------\n",
    "\n",
    "Example:\n",
    "\n",
    "Location: https://example.com/login\n",
    "\n",
    "It tells the client the URI where a resource can be found,\n",
    "commonly after a redirect.\n",
    "\n",
    "--------------------------------------------------\n",
    "2. CONTENT-LOCATION HEADER\n",
    "--------------------------------------------------\n",
    "\n",
    "It identifies the URI of the specific representation/content\n",
    "being returned.\n",
    "\n",
    "Example:\n",
    "\n",
    "Content-Location: /documents/report.html\n",
    "\n",
    "--------------------------------------------------\n",
    "3. SECURITY ISSUE\n",
    "\n",
    "If one organization/server can control these URI values\n",
    "without proper validation, it may cause operations such as\n",
    "cache invalidation to affect resources belonging to another\n",
    "organization.\n",
    "\n",
    "Therefore, servers should:\n",
    "\n",
    "    • Validate Location and Content-Location values\n",
    "    • Ensure they refer only to resources within the\n",
    "      appropriate authority/trust boundary\n",
    "\n",
    "--------------------------------------------------\n",
    "MEMORY TRICK\n",
    "\n",
    "Location\n",
    "    → Where should the client go?\n",
    "\n",
    "Content-Location\n",
    "    → Where is this particular content/representation identified?\n",
    "\n",
    "Main security idea:\n",
    "    Validate URI → Prevent cross-organization resource impact"
   ]
  }
 ],
